 "reviewHtml": "<div><p>The applause rose and rose in a manner that usually indicates one of two things: (a) You have just witnessed a brilliant show. (b) You have just seen a show whose audience is so sympathetic toward the designers that its badness only makes them clap harder. Deanna and Laura Fanning have delivered some shows that were borderline a’s in the past. Today was a definite b.</p><p>The problem was an overconceived, performatively abstruse concept that had little chance of working because not enough thought had gone into its production or consideration of its function. The result was a show that took itself dead seriously while almost absurdly failing to meet the first criterion of a fashion show.</p><p>Nanda Vigo once told Hans Ulrich Obrist: “What I wish to obtain by refracting light is that the viewer is induced, almost forced, to think of the light.” The Italian artist was the Fanning sisters’ inspiration today, and her works—the lights and chairs—were scattered through a runway space divided into sections. I can only speak for my section, but it contained two of Vigo’s severe-looking chairs and a narrow cone of spotlit illumination. Apart from the dull glow of a Vigo light structure on the wall and the green glow of an emergency exit sign, all else was darkness. I thought of the light.</p><p>The models moved in and out of the space, most often avoiding the spotlit patch to loom through the gloom. They had been instructed, it seemed, to improvise emotional states. Sometimes you would glimpse a look as it moved beneath the dust-strafed spotlight in front of you; more often the clothes passed through the inky dusk. The most lucid moments of illumination revealed a lush, vaguely 1940s red coat with broad angled shoulders; translucent striped knitwear; gray tailoring with wide side-pleated trousers; a knit jumpsuit with retrofuturist pointed shoulders; and a dress printed with diffused traffic lights beneath metallic-tinted gauze. There were also skirts with pixie-ish zigzag hems. It seemed to go on for a long time.</p><p>I thought of the light. But in the absence of much light to see by, other senses took over. Highlights included the funky abstract xylophone, Vigo’s assertive statements in Italian, and the haphazard thumps of the heavy glass-cube keepsakes left on our benches (apparently unnoticed by many) as they fell to the floor.</p><p>The photo gallery should reveal this collection pretty well, but schlepping there to see it today proved a waste of time. Still you can’t be experimental without failing sometimes, and that’s OK.</p></div>",
